isms`\n    *   **Unit Catalog:** 3 units (`Ch3_Mechanisms:1`, `Ch3_Mechanisms:2`, `Ch3_Mechanisms:3`). Each has paragraph briefs, cases, case suggestions, source handles.\n    *   **Source Routing/Materials:** Provided extensive routing and material summaries for many papers (P0284 to P0414). I need to select available literature cases for the writing units based on the unit catalog and source materials.\n    *   **Constraints:** \n        *   Target ~150-200 independent lit for the whole review (not per chapter/batch). This batch just adds to specific units.\n        *   Don't repeat existing sources. Add new ones that supplement different results, conditions, methods, developmental stages, or useful contrasts. If only repeating same generalization, leave in pool, additions can be empty.\n        *   Write `proposed_use`: ~30-60 words explaining what this material helps explain in this unit (proposed use), can propose a synthesis. This is for writing, not paper results. Methods/results/conclusions come from attached A/B/deep reads. Recommended use cannot misattribute analogies/schemes/other objects' results as direct empirical evidence for this problem. `material_available` false means no actual content, don't fabricate.\n        *   Output contract: JSON object with `additions` array. Each item: `unit_key`, `studies` array (`source_handle`, `proposed_use`; compatible with `contribution` field name).\n        *   Focus on substantive BODY tasks only. Develop deep teaching, analysis, methods, mechanisms and evidence fully regardless of publication heading. Do not return or modify manuscript_parts_plan.\n\n2.  **Map Units to Needs & Select Sources:**\n    *   **Unit 1 (`Ch3_Mechanisms:1`):** Focuses on metabolic regulation (Inosine inhibiting UBA6, SCFAs/Tryptophan derivatives/Bile acids via epigenetic/GPCR/AhR pathways, dose/tissue specificity). Needs sources that support metabolic mechanisms, dual roles, concentration dependence, and specific receptor/pathway details.\n        *   *Existing cases:* P0582, P0617, P0635, P0486, P0447, P0357.\n        *   *Suggestions in catalog:* P0075, P0092, P0220, P0282, P0180.\n        *   *Available high-quality matches from routing/materials:*\n            *   `P0326` (Microbiome-derived metabolome as potential predictor): Discusses SCFA levels correlating with response, mentions contradictory results (low butyrate better for anti-CTLA-4 vs high for PD-1), proposes functional metabolome over taxonomy. Good for context-dependence/dual role.\n            *   `P0369` (Gut microbiota–derived metabolites in immunomodulation and GI cancer): Details SCFAs, bile acids, tryptophan derivatives via HDAC inhibition, GPCR, AhR, m6A. Explains bidirectional/context-dependent effects. Perfect for Unit 1's second point.\n            *   `P0413` (Effect of Gut Microbiota-Derived Metabolites on ICI Therapy: Enemy or Friend?): Explicitly covers SCFAs, inosine, TMAO, tryptophan, bile acids. Highlights dual roles (e.g., butyrate beneficial for PD-1 but detrimental for CTLA-4), detailed molecular mechanisms (HDAC, GPR, AhR, PERK/Gasdermin-E). Excellent for mechanistic depth and context-dependence.\n            *   `P0391` (Programming the tumor microenvironment through microbiome-driven mechanisms): Framework for \"microbiome-driven TME programming\", details metabolite-receptor-TME component outcomes, discusses double-edged nature of butyrate, bile acids, etc. Good for systemic/local signaling framework.\n        *   *Selection for Unit 1:* P0369, P0413, P0326, P0391. I'll draft proposed uses focusing on metabolic pathways, dose/context dependency, and dual roles.\n\n    *   **Unit 2 (`Ch3_Mechanisms:2`):** Focuses on bacterial translocation/migration to lymph nodes bridging innate/adaptive immunity, promoting CD8+ T cell homing; and antigenic mimicry hypothesis (microbial peptides homologous to tumor neoantigens inducing cross-reactive T cells/antibodies).\n        *   *Existing cases:* P0114, P0587, P0113, P